{
  "slug": "usos-de-se",
  "number": 23,
  "part": 5,
  "level": "B1",
  "title": "The many uses of se",
  "tagline": "Se habla español, se vende, se me cayó: impersonal, passive and accidental se.",
  "goal": "Recognize and use se for general statements, the passive, unplanned accidents, and as a replacement for le before lo / la.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "impersonal",
      "title": "Impersonal and passive se",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*Se* + verb in the 3rd person talks about what **people in general** do, or what **is done**, without saying who. It's far more common than the passive with *ser*."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Aquí {se habla} español.",
              "tr": "Spanish is spoken here."
            },
            {
              "fr": "{Se vende} casa.",
              "tr": "House for sale."
            },
            {
              "fr": "{Se venden} casas.",
              "tr": "Houses for sale.",
              "note": "the verb agrees with the thing"
            },
            {
              "fr": "¿Cómo {se dice} «weekend» en español?",
              "tr": "How do you say «weekend» in Spanish?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "En México {se come} tarde.",
              "tr": "In Mexico people eat late."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "accidental",
      "title": "Accidents: se me cayó",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For unplanned events, Spanish makes the **thing** the subject and the person an indirect object. It sounds less like your fault: «it fell (on me)»."
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "se + me / te / le / nos / les + verbo (agrees with the thing)"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Spanish",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*{Se me cayó} el celular.*",
              "I dropped my phone."
            ],
            [
              "*{Se me olvidaron} las llaves.*",
              "I forgot my keys."
            ],
            [
              "*{Se nos acabó} la leche.*",
              "We ran out of milk."
            ],
            [
              "*{Se le rompió} la pantalla.*",
              "His screen broke."
            ],
            [
              "*{Se me perdió} la cartera.*",
              "I lost my wallet."
            ],
            [
              "*{Se nos descompuso} el carro.*",
              "Our car broke down."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "otros",
      "title": "Two more se's",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Reflexive and reciprocal** [[10]]: *Se levanta temprano. Se quieren mucho.*",
            "**Le / les before lo / la** [[15]]: *Le doy el libro → {Se lo} doy.*"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "When you see *se*, ask: is somebody doing it to themselves (reflexive), to each other (reciprocal), is it «people / it is done» (impersonal), or did something just happen (accidental)?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
